# Use case: crime along the streets of Tempe, Arizona

## Setup

> Networkex, GeoFrame and Spatex come from Hex. To use a local checkout of Networkex instead (to
> try changes that are not released yet), set `NETWORKEX_PATH` to it before starting Livebook; a
> checkout builds its NIF from source, which needs Rust. From the root of the checkout:
>
> ```
> NETWORKEX_PATH=$PWD livebook server notebooks/tempe_case.livemd
> ```
>
> or headlessly, to check that every cell works:
>
> ```
> NETWORKEX_PATH=$PWD elixir scripts/run_livemd.exs notebooks/tempe_case.livemd
> ```
>
> Spanish version: `notebooks/es/caso_tempe.livemd`.

```elixir
# NETWORKEX_PATH: a local checkout of Networkex to use instead of the Hex package. A checkout
# builds its NIF from source, which needs Rustler.
networkex =
  case System.get_env("NETWORKEX_PATH") do
    path when path in [nil, ""] -> [{:networkex, "~> 0.3"}]
    path -> [{:networkex, path: Path.expand(path)}, {:rustler, "~> 0.38", runtime: false}]
  end

Mix.install(
  networkex ++
    [
      {:geoframe, "~> 0.3.4"},
      {:spatex, "~> 0.2.1"},
      {:explorer, "~> 0.13"},
      {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
      {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"},
      {:req, "~> 0.5"}
    ]
)
```

The data are in `notebooks/data`, next to this notebook in the repository. Opened from anywhere
else, such as with "Run in Livebook" on HexDocs, the notebook downloads them once from the
repository on GitHub, at the tag of the installed Networkex version.

```elixir
# A Shapefile layer is its .shp, .shx, .dbf and .prj files.
shapefile = fn layer -> for ext <- ~w(shp shx dbf prj), do: "#{layer}.#{ext}" end

data_dir = fn name, files ->
  local = Path.join([__DIR__, "data", name])

  if File.dir?(local) do
    local
  else
    vsn = Application.spec(:networkex, :vsn)
    repo = "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CountlinkX-Solutions/networkex"
    cache = Path.join([System.tmp_dir!(), "networkex-#{vsn}", name])
    File.mkdir_p!(cache)

    for file <- files, not File.exists?(Path.join(cache, file)) do
      url = "#{repo}/v#{vsn}/notebooks/data/#{name}/#{file}"
      %{body: body} = Req.get!(url, decode_body: false, http_errors: :raise)
      File.write!(Path.join(cache, file), body)
    end

    cache
  end
end
```

## The question

Crime happens on streets. A map of incidents invites the usual questions (are they clustered?
how far are they from schools?), but measuring them "as the crow flies" ignores that people,
cars and patrols move along the street network: two incidents on either side of a block may be
far apart for anyone on foot. Network analysis measures along the streets instead.

The data are the spaghetti tutorials' Tempe, Arizona sample (distributed with PySAL): 293 street
lines, 287 crimes and 8 schools, in StatePlane Arizona Central (US feet). We ask:

1. What does the street **network** look like (segments, intersections, connectivity)?
2. How far is each crime from the **nearest school**, along the streets?
3. Are crimes **clustered along the network**, beyond what streets alone would produce?
4. Do streets with many crimes neighbour streets with many crimes (**spatial autocorrelation**)?
5. Which **routes** lead from the crimes to their nearest school?

## 1. The street network

```elixir
require Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF
alias Explorer.Series, as: S
alias GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame, as: GDF
alias Networkex.Network

dir = data_dir.("geodanet", Enum.flat_map(~w(streets crimes schools), shapefile))

# The layers carry the CRS of their .prj files, StatePlane Arizona Central (NAD 1983) in US
# feet, so every distance below is in US feet.
read = fn name -> dir |> Path.join(name) |> GeoFrame.read_file!() end

streets = read.("streets.shp")
crimes = read.("crimes.shp")
schools = read.("schools.shp")
crs = GDF.crs(streets)

{GDF.n_rows(streets), GDF.n_rows(crimes), GDF.n_rows(schools)}
```

`Networkex.GeoFrame.network/2` turns every line into **arcs** between consecutive vertices, and
joins lines that meet at the same (rounded) point. The **graph** merges every chain of
degree-2 vertices into one **edge**, from intersection to intersection.

```elixir
ntw = Networkex.GeoFrame.network(streets)

%{
  vertices: length(ntw.vertices),
  arcs: length(ntw.arcs),
  graph_edges: length(ntw.graph.edges),
  components: ntw.network.n_components,
  total_length_miles: Enum.sum(ntw.arc_lengths) / 5280
}
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.deck(Networkex.GeoFrame.arcs(ntw, crs: crs),
  color: {"length", :viridis},
  tooltip: ["arc", "length"]
)
```

## 2. Crimes and schools on the network

Each observation is **snapped** to the nearest point of its nearest street segment. The snapping
distance says how far the recorded location lies from the street.

```elixir
crime_pp = Networkex.GeoFrame.snap(ntw, crimes)
school_pp = Networkex.GeoFrame.snap(ntw, schools)

snap = S.from_list(crime_pp.dist_snapped)
%{max_snap_ft: S.max(snap), mean_snap_ft: S.mean(snap)}
```

The network distance from each crime to its nearest school, next to the straight-line distance:
the ratio tells how much longer the trip along the streets is.

```elixir
nearest = Network.nearest_neighbor_distances(ntw, crime_pp, school_pp)

straight = fn {x1, y1}, {x2, y2} -> :math.sqrt((x2 - x1) ** 2 + (y2 - y1) ** 2) end

table =
  Enum.zip(crime_pp.snapped, nearest)
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.map(fn {{p, {[school | _], network_ft}}, i} ->
    euclid = straight.(p, Enum.at(school_pp.snapped, school))
    %{crime: i, school: school, network_ft: network_ft, straight_ft: euclid, ratio: network_ft / euclid}
  end)
  |> DF.new()

DF.summarise(table, mean_network_ft: mean(network_ft), mean_ratio: mean(ratio), max_ratio: max(ratio))
```

```elixir
school_of = table |> DF.pull("school") |> S.to_list()

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(Networkex.GeoFrame.points(crime_pp, columns: %{"nearest_school" => school_of}, crs: crs),
  color: {"nearest_school", :categorical},
  radius: 4,
  tooltip: ["nearest_school", "snap_distance"]
)
```

## 3. Are crimes clustered along the streets?

The global auto **K-function** counts, for growing distances `r`, how many pairs of crimes are
closer than `r` along the network, relative to the crime intensity. It is compared with the same
curve for random patterns of as many points spread uniformly along the streets: if the observed
curve rises above the simulation envelope, crimes are closer to each other than the street layout
alone explains.

```elixir
k = Network.global_auto_k(ntw, crime_pp, nsteps: 20, permutations: 99, seed: 7, threshold: 0.05)

curves =
  for {r, obs, lo, hi} <- Enum.zip([k.xaxis, k.observed, k.lower_envelope, k.upper_envelope]),
      {name, v} <- [observed: obs, lower: lo, upper: hi],
      do: %{distance_ft: r, curve: Atom.to_string(name), k: v}

VegaLite.new(width: 520, height: 300, title: "Global auto K along the network")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(curves)
|> VegaLite.mark(:line)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "distance_ft", type: :quantitative, title: "distance (ft)")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "k", type: :quantitative, title: "K(r)")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "curve", type: :nominal)
```

```elixir
above =
  Enum.zip([k.xaxis, k.observed, k.upper_envelope])
  |> Enum.filter(fn {_r, obs, up} -> obs > up end)
  |> Enum.map(fn {r, _, _} -> round(r) end)

%{distances_above_envelope_ft: above}
```

spaghetti's envelope scales the extreme simulated values (by `1 - threshold / 2` and
`threshold / 2`); `threshold: 0.05` keeps them almost as they are. The observed curve is above
the envelope from about 500 feet up to a little over 3000 feet, and again from about 6400 feet:
crimes are closer to each other along the streets than 287 random points on the same streets
would be, at block scale and at the scale of the whole area.

## 4. Crimes per street and spatial autocorrelation

Counting crimes per arc gives a value per street segment. Two segments are neighbours when they
share a vertex. Moran's I (from Spatex, on Networkex's contiguity weights) then asks whether
segments with many crimes sit next to each other.

```elixir
counts = Network.counts_per_link(ntw, crime_pp)
w = Network.contiguity_weights(ntw)
graph = Spatex.Graph.from_arrays(w.focal, w.neighbor, w.weight, ids: Enum.to_list(0..(w.n - 1)))
moran = Spatex.ESDA.moran(counts, graph, permutations: 999, seed: 1)

%{moran_i: moran.i, expected: moran.ei, p_sim: moran.p_sim}
```

Moran's I is about 0.005, against an expected -0.003, with a pseudo p-value around 0.3: no
evidence that segments with many crimes are next to segments with many crimes. The clustering
found by the K-function is a matter of crimes being near one another, not of whole groups of
neighbouring streets standing out.

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.deck(Networkex.GeoFrame.arcs(ntw, columns: %{"crimes" => counts}, crs: crs),
  color: {"crimes", :viridis},
  width: "crimes",
  tooltip: ["arc", "crimes", "length"]
)
```

Arcs are as long as the digitised segments, so a long arc collects more crimes just by its length.
Splitting the network into pieces of at most 500 feet makes the units comparable; the crimes
are snapped again to the split network.

```elixir
split = Network.split_arcs(ntw, 500)
split_pp = Networkex.GeoFrame.snap(split, crimes)
split_counts = Network.counts_per_link(split, split_pp)
sw = Network.contiguity_weights(split)

split_moran =
  Spatex.ESDA.moran(
    split_counts,
    Spatex.Graph.from_arrays(sw.focal, sw.neighbor, sw.weight, ids: Enum.to_list(0..(sw.n - 1))),
    permutations: 999,
    seed: 1
  )

%{arcs: length(split.arcs), moran_i: split_moran.i, p_sim: split_moran.p_sim}
```

## 5. Routes to the nearest school

The shortest path from a few crimes to their nearest school, along the streets:

```elixir
picked = [0, 50, 100, 150, 200]

routes =
  for i <- picked do
    one = %{
      crime_pp
      | points: [Enum.at(crime_pp.points, i)],
        snapped: [Enum.at(crime_pp.snapped, i)],
        arcs: [Enum.at(crime_pp.arcs, i)],
        dist_to_vertex: [Enum.at(crime_pp.dist_to_vertex, i)],
        dist_snapped: [Enum.at(crime_pp.dist_snapped, i)]
    }

    school = Enum.at(school_of, i)
    Network.shortest_paths(ntw, one, school_pp) |> Enum.at(school) |> Map.put(:pair, {i, school})
  end

paths = Networkex.GeoFrame.paths(routes, crs: crs)
DF.new(%{crime: Enum.map(routes, &elem(&1.pair, 0)), school: Enum.map(routes, &elem(&1.pair, 1)), length_ft: S.to_list(GDF.pull(paths, "length"))})
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.deck(paths, color: {"dest", :categorical}, width: 4, tooltip: ["source", "dest", "length"])
```

The path lengths agree with the network distances of section 2 to about a millionth of a percent
(the arc lengths run from each rounded vertex to the next vertex as digitised, the paths join the
rounded vertices): the path is the route those distances measure.

## What we learned

* The 293 street lines form one connected network of 230 vertices and 303 arcs (179 edges between
  intersections), about 19.8 miles of streets.
* Recorded crime locations lie on average 90 feet from the street (at most 326). Along the streets
  the nearest school is on average 1500 feet away, a third more than in a straight line, and up to
  2.6 times the straight distance.
* The network K-function shows crimes clustered along the streets beyond what the street layout
  explains, both at short distances and over the whole area.
* Counts per segment show no spatial autocorrelation by network contiguity (Moran's I near its
  expectation), also with segments split to at most 500 feet.

Every Networkex function used here is verified against spaghetti (see the parity guide).
